---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import Gloss from '../../components/Gloss.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const ordinary = assertion('cancer-is-the-ordinary-half')
const men = assertion('the-cancer-burden-is-a-mens-burden')
const rank = assertion('a-county-rank-comes-with-an-interval')
const gap = assertion('cancer-is-not-where-the-racial-gap-lives')

const spec = (a: typeof ordinary, yTitle: string) =>
  bar(
    a.figures.map((f) => f.label),
    a.figures.map((f) => f.value),
    { yTitle, labelValues: true },
  )

const sitesSpec = spec(ordinary, 'Deaths a year')
const sexSpec = spec(men, 'Deaths per 100,000')
const gapSpec = spec(gap, 'Points, Black minus white')
---

<Article slug="the-ordinary-half">
  <p>
    Allen County dies young, and it does so on every measure taken of it: 74.3 years of life
    expectancy against 77.1 for the country, more years lost before seventy-five than the state or
    the nation, and a heart disease death rate a third above Ohio's. So the obvious next
    question is what else is killing people here, and the obvious first candidate is cancer.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is not. Cancer kills about 220 people a year in this county at a rate within a point of
    Ohio's, and the rate is falling.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={ordinary} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="sites"
    title="The four cancers Allen County loses most people to each year"
    caption="Average annual deaths, 2019–2023. Fifteen sites have a published rate here and four — stomach, cervix, uterus and thyroid — are suppressed because the county has three or fewer deaths a year from each."
    columns={['Site', 'Deaths a year']}
    rows={ordinary.figures.map((f) => [f.label, String(f.value)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="sites" set:html={JSON.stringify(sitesSpec)} is:inline />
  <Gloss>
    The agency that holds the death certificates will not give a county to a machine, which is why
    this site knew the county's heart disease and stroke rates and nothing else it dies of. The
    National Cancer Institute publishes about twenty causes at county grain through a different
    door.
  </Gloss>
  <p>
    There is also less cancer here than in Ohio — 459 new cases per 100,000 against the state's 473
    — and about as much dying of it. Diabetes and chronic lower respiratory disease are still
    unmeasured at this grain, so the answer to what else kills people here is not complete. But the
    largest candidate has been checked and it is ordinary.
  </p>

  <h2>Except that it is a men's disease here</h2>
  <p>
    Split by sex the county stops being average. Men die of cancer here at 201.9 per 100,000, above
    Ohio's men and eighteen per cent above American men. Women die at 129.6, below Ohio's women and
    close to the national rate. Of 220 cancer deaths a year, 124 are men.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={men} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="sex"
    title="Cancer death rates by sex, Allen County and Ohio"
    caption="Age-adjusted deaths per 100,000, 2019–2023. The national rates are 171.5 for men and 126.3 for women. The county's men rank 43rd of Ohio's 88 counties and its women 74th."
    columns={['Group', 'Deaths per 100,000']}
    rows={men.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(1)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="sex" set:html={JSON.stringify(sexSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    The sites tell the same story. Of fifteen with a published rate, the county exceeds the national
    rate meaningfully on three — lung, oesophagus and pancreas — and sits below it on colon and
    rectum, breast, liver and leukemia. Those three are the ones a tobacco explanation would
    predict, and CDC estimates that 17.0 per cent of the county's adults and 22.8 per cent of Lima's
    smoke. This site is not joining the two: it has no exposure history for this county, no latency
    model, and nothing to compare against.
  </p>

  <h2>A rank is an estimate</h2>
  <p>
    The most-repeated sentence on this site is a rank. Fifth worst of 88 for heart disease. Rank 82
    for stroke. First in Ohio for reported toxic releases. Every one of them was made by sorting
    eighty-eight numbers and reading off a position, and every one was printed as an integer.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={rank} />
  <p>
    The cancer source publishes the interval on its own ranks, and that is how large the uncertainty
    turns out to be. Sixty-second of eighty-eight means somewhere between twenty-ninth and eightieth
    — which is to say, somewhere in the middle three-quarters, which is what having no idea looks
    like in rank form. Sixteenth for pancreatic cancer, on nineteen deaths a year, means anywhere
    from second to seventy-third.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the ratio is the sentence worth trusting and the rank is the sentence worth reading with
    care. A rate 1.35 times the state's rests on two estimates. A rank rests on eighty-eight and
    shows none of them. The heart-disease finding is not withdrawn — that ratio is large and the
    rank is probably in the worst tenth — but it now says on its face that it was sorted rather than
    counted.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the gap is not</h2>
  <p>
    This county's Black residents live 6.91 fewer years than its white residents, a gap wider than
    Ohio's 5.48 and the nation's 5.08. That is the largest single fact this site holds about health
    here, and every cause it examines is a candidate explanation.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={gap} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="gap"
    title="The racial gap in cancer death rates, three geographies"
    caption="Black non-Hispanic minus white non-Hispanic age-adjusted rate, deaths per 100,000, 2019–2023. Allen County's gap is the narrowest of the three, and both of its figures are below Ohio's for the same group."
    columns={['Geography', 'Gap, points']}
    rows={gap.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(1)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="gap" set:html={JSON.stringify(gapSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Cancer is not one. The gap here is 8.3 points where Ohio's is 11.6 and the nation's 15.3, and
    both of the county's own figures sit below the state's for the same group. This is the second
    cause to be checked against that gap and the second to come back narrower than expected — the
    heart-disease record found a gap that was large and entirely ordinary for Ohio. Whatever makes
    this county's racial life-expectancy gap the widest of the three, two of the biggest killers
    have now been asked and neither answers.
  </p>
</Article>
